{
  "image_name": "5fb80877e54d2f35ca3e6526a6729128.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how solvent polarity controls whether an external electric field can reach the active “core” of a molecular device. In the top row, a core is surrounded either by nonpolar molecules or by polar molecules. In a nonpolar solvent, the surrounding molecules do not align strongly, so the field at the core is effectively absent in the no-field case and can remain unscreened when a field is applied. In a polar solvent, however, molecular dipoles orient with the field. If those dipoles are disordered there is still no net field at the core, but when they become ordered, their collective polarization generates a compensating internal field, so the core experiences a field equal and opposite to the solvent response.\n\nThe bottom row applies this idea to a source–linker–core–linker–drain molecular junction under an oriented external electric field (OEEF). In polar solvent, aligned solvent dipoles around the junction screen or quench the field at the core, weakening any field-driven effect. In nonpolar solvent, little screening occurs, so the external field penetrates to the core. Overall, the message is that molecular order in polar media suppresses OEEF action, whereas nonpolar environments preserve it.",
